1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,160
Try-with-resources made opening and
closing honest. The next question is what
we open when the thing we care about is a
path on disk.

2
00:00:08,279 --> 00:00:20,160
Suppose a batch job must write a short
status file after each run and read
yesterday's status at startup. Older Java
pushed you toward java.io.File plus
streams glued together by hand. It works.

3
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:34,240
It is also noisy, easy to get charset-
wrong, and awkward for walking a tree or
moving a file into place atomically. What
is the modern way Java talks to the
filesystem? NIO.2 answers with Path and
Files.

4
00:00:34,359 --> 00:00:48,240
Prefer them for new code. File is legacy
you will still meet in maintenance; it is
not the model you should reach for first.
Look at this java for a moment.
Path.of(...) builds a location, not the
bytes themselves.

5
00:00:48,359 --> 00:01:01,359
Creating parent directories makes the
write robust when the folder structure is
part of the job. Files.writeString and
Files.readString hide boilerplate while
still throwing clear I/O exceptions when
the world disagrees.

6
00:01:01,479 --> 00:01:10,519
Combined with try-with-resources for
streams when you need finer control, this
is the everyday filesystem vocabulary.
Charset is the quiet requirement inside
those helpers.

7
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:22,000
Text is not "just bytes with letters on
them." Assume the default charset, and a
machine in another locale — or a container
with a different default — can reinterpret
your file: Look at this java for a moment.

8
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:29,200
When you drop to stream APIs, carry the
same discipline: pick an intentional
charset instead of hoping defaults match
production.

9
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:38,320
Walking appears as soon as "one file"
becomes "a directory of inputs": Look at
this java for a moment. Files.walk gives
you a stream of paths.

10
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:51,600
Close the stream — try-with-resources
again — because walking can hold directory
resources. Express filesystem intent
without hand-rolling recursion badly.
Atomic move matters the first time you
publish a file safely.

11
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:58,840
Write to a temporary sibling, then move
into the final name so readers never see a
half-written file: Look at this java for a
moment.

12
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:04,080
On some filesystems, "atomic move" has
constraints — especially across storage
volumes.

13
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:14,520
Treat atomic rename as a tool with
documented limits, not as a spell that
always works the way your laptop's disk
did in development. Path traversal is the
security sibling.

14
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:26,840
If any part of a path comes from user
input, ../../etc/passwd is not a cute edge
case — it is an attack. Resolve and
normalize against a known root, and reject
paths that escape it.

15
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:39,120
NIO.2 makes path math clearer; it does not
make untrusted input safe by itself.
Common mistakes line up with those
pressures. Default charset corrupts text
across environments.

16
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:48,439
Not handling NoSuchFileException turns a
missing input into an opaque crash at the
wrong layer. Blindly concatenating user
strings into paths invites traversal.

17
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:56,599
What if we keep using new File("data.txt")
and manual streams for every new feature?
You can ship.

18
00:02:56,719 --> 00:03:08,680
You will also keep re-solving charset,
recursive listing, and move semantics with
one-off utilities that disagree with each
other. Path and Files concentrate those
answers in one modern vocabulary.

19
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:22,560
Existence checks, size, and copy read as
ordinary operations — and that readability
is part of correctness, because reviewers
can see intent without decoding a ritual
of streams for a simple copy.

20
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:32,040
Prefer NIO.2 for new code even when a
teammate reaches for File out of habit.
Translation layers between Path and File
should be boundaries, not the center of
new design.

21
00:03:32,159 --> 00:03:42,719
One more habit: Files.deleteIfExists is
intentionally forgiving; Files.delete is
not. Choosing between them is a product
decision about whether absence is normal.

22
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:55,759
NIO.2 gives you the verbs — your domain
decides which verb matches the invariant.
And yet reading and writing "strings"
still hides a deeper split: sometimes the
payload is text, and sometimes it is raw
bytes.

23
00:03:55,879 --> 00:04:00,240
Bridging those worlds — and doing it
efficiently — is the next pressure.
